NAME
       curses - terminal windows, attributes and keys

SYNOPSIS
       #include <curses.h>

       WINDOW *initscr(void);
       int     endwin(void);

       WINDOW *newwin(int h, int w, int y, int x);
       int     delwin(WINDOW *w);

       int wrefresh(WINDOW *w);
       int wnoutrefresh(WINDOW *w);
       int doupdate(void);

       int mvwaddstr(WINDOW *w, int y, int x, const char *s);
       int mvwprintw(WINDOW *w, int y, int x, const char *fmt, ...);
       int wattrset(WINDOW *w, attr_t a);
       int box(WINDOW *w, chtype v, chtype h);

       int start_color(void);
       int init_pair(short pair, short fg, short bg);

       int keypad(WINDOW *w, bool_t on);
       int wgetch(WINDOW *w);

DESCRIPTION
       A curses implementation for Cervus, following the familiar
       interface closely enough that ordinary curses code compiles.

       initscr measures the terminal, switches to the alternate screen
       and puts the terminal in raw mode without echo; endwin puts all
       of that back. Call endwin before exiting, including from an
       error path, or the terminal is left unusable.

       Drawing goes into windows, not to the terminal. wrefresh copies
       a window into the virtual screen and then updates the terminal.
       When several windows change together, call wnoutrefresh on each
       and doupdate once, so the screen is written a single time.

       The update compares the virtual screen against what the terminal
       already shows and emits only the cells that differ, together
       with the cursor moves and attribute changes they need. Redrawing
       a full screen where little changed costs almost nothing, which
       matters on a console that renders every glyph itself.

ATTRIBUTES AND COLOR
       A_NORMAL, A_BOLD, A_DIM, A_UNDERLINE, A_REVERSE and A_BLINK
       combine with a colour pair. start_color enables colour and
       init_pair defines a pair from a foreground and a background out
       of COLOR_BLACK through COLOR_WHITE; COLOR_PAIR(n) turns the pair
       into an attribute, and PAIR_NUMBER extracts it again.

       Attributes set with wattrset apply to everything drawn
       afterwards in that window.

INPUT
       With keypad turned on, wgetch decodes escape sequences and
       returns KEY_UP, KEY_DOWN, KEY_LEFT, KEY_RIGHT, KEY_HOME,
       KEY_END, KEY_PPAGE, KEY_NPAGE, KEY_DC, KEY_IC, KEY_BACKSPACE,
       KEY_ENTER, and the function keys as KEY_F(n).

       By default wgetch waits. nodelay makes it return ERR at once
       when nothing is ready, and wtimeout or halfdelay wait for a
       bounded time, which is how a program animates or refreshes on a
       timer while staying responsive.

       A read interrupted by a signal is reported as KEY_RESIZE, so a
       program that redraws on that key also repaints when the console
       hands the screen back after a switch.

NOTES
       Enter arrives as a newline and Backspace as a backspace rather
       than delete; wgetch normalises both to KEY_ENTER and
       KEY_BACKSPACE when keypad is on, so compare against those.

       There is no terminfo database. The escape sequences are the ones
       the Cervus console implements.

SEE ALSO
       termios(3), tui(3), poll(2), hexed(1)
